sensus", "The two most recent polls show Pulido leading by five to six points, a shift from Republican-leaning 2025 polling", "The district's R+7 lean, incumbency, and De La Cruz's cash advantage keep most raters at Tilt/Lean Republican", "A Democratic-friendly national environment and redistricting friction among South Texas Latino voters"], "rationale": "Most raters still tilt Republican given the R+7 district and the incumbent's fundraising edge, but both prediction markets price Pulido near 73% and the most recent polls show him ahead by five to six points after trailing in 2025. That tension lands the race essentially even with a slight Democratic edge and wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Fresh nonpartisan polling this fall and movement in the South Texas Latino vote could resolve whether the markets or the raters are reading this district correctly.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Monica De La Cruz (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Texas's 15th District pairs Republican incumbent Monica De La Cruz with Democratic challenger Bobby Pulido in territory reshaped by the state's 2025 redistricting. The seat's partisan lean favors Republicans by about seven points, and De La Cruz has out-raised Pulido with a clear cash-on-hand advantage, which is why most outside raters still call the race Tilt or Lean Republican.\n\nRecent polling points the other way: an August survey of likely voters put Pulido up five, and both major prediction markets price his chances near seventy percent. A favorable national environment for Democrats and signs of shifting support among South Texas Latino voters help explain the disconnect. The race sits close to even with modest Democratic momentum, and uncertainty remains high because the polling is thin and partly partisan.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4454, "margin": -0.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6234, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6548, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p